From: h713 <h713@local>
Date: Thu, 28 Aug 2026 19:20:00 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] EXPERIMENT: identity-map the adopted scanout so master 2 can
 be translated

Pairs with 0068 and 0069, and exists because 0069 alone produced a confounded
boot. Out of series with them.

THE PROBLEM 0069 RAN INTO. Attaching dec@5600000 to IOMMU master 2 with
translation on faults immediately:

  platform 5600000.dec: Adding to iommu group 0
  sun50i-iommu 2010000.iommu: Page fault for 0x000000006c332000 (master 2, rd)

0x6c332000 is inside U-Boot's scanout carveout at 0x6c100000. Master 2 is the
display's own fetch path, and Linux adopts a display that U-Boot already
programmed with a *physical* source address. The moment that master starts
translating, the address the hardware is still fetching stops resolving.

The damage is not limited to the logo. A control on that boot repointed the
live OSD channel at DECD's own valid, mapped IOVA, saw the READY latch consumed
and no fault, and still got a black panel -- while vsync held 59.7 Hz. One
translation fault appears to wedge the AFBD fetch engine for the rest of the
boot. So 0069's black screen said nothing about DECD routing, and could not.

WHAT THIS DOES. Give the domain an identity mapping for the carveout the
adopted scanout is fetching, so translation is transparent to it:

  - framebuf_reserved gains `iommu-addresses = <&dec 0x6c100000 0x800000>`.
    of_iommu_get_resv_regions() walks the device's `memory-region` list, and
    for each entry whose `iommu-addresses` phandle points back at the device
    it builds a reserved region. Because the requested IOVA equals the node's
    own `reg` base and the lengths match, iommu_resv_region_get_type() classes
    it IOMMU_RESV_DIRECT -- an identity mapping -- rather than a bare
    reservation.
  - dec@5600000 lists that node in `memory-region` so the walk finds it. It
    stays at index 1: the driver takes `of_parse_phandle(..., "memory-region",
    0)` for its own frame carveout, which is unchanged at index 0.
  - sun50i-iommu gains `.get_resv_regions = iommu_dma_get_resv_regions`, the
    standard helper that forwards to of_iommu_get_resv_regions(). The driver
    had no get_resv_regions op at all, so the core had nothing to apply and
    reserved regions were silently inert. This is the piece that makes the DT
    property mean anything. exynos-iommu.c is the pattern being followed,
    including the private "dma-iommu.h" include.

The core does the rest: iommu_create_device_direct_mappings() maps
IOMMU_RESV_DIRECT regions into the default domain when the device attaches,
before anything else touches it.

WHAT SUCCESS LOOKS LIKE. The U-Boot logo survives the boot -- that alone is the
result, because it means a translated master 2 no longer breaks the display
Linux inherited. No page fault at attach. Then DECD frames can be submitted
with the fetch engine in a known-good state, and a black panel would finally
mean something about routing rather than about a wedged engine.

WHAT THIS IS NOT. It is not what stock does. Stock owns the display from reset
and never has a foreign physical scanout address live at attach time, so it
needs no such mapping. This is scaffolding for making the adopted-display model
testable under translation, not a step toward the vendor configuration. If the
no-GPU route ever ships, the honest form is Linux programming the display
itself, and then this patch is unnecessary.

RUN ON HARDWARE 2026-08-28. It works, and it made the DECD question answerable
for the first time.

The first cut of this patch panicked on every boot, which is how the driver bug
above was found. With the fix in place:

  platform 5600000.dec: Adding to iommu group 0
  (no page fault)
  decd 5600000.dec: reconstructed decd probed

Zero faults, zero panics, userspace up -- and the operator confirmed the U-Boot
logo was still on the panel, with master 2 translating. That is the success
criterion stated above, met.

Then, all on that one boot, with the fetch engine demonstrably healthy:

  DECD holding a red NV12 frame   60 IRQ/s, Y=0xFFE00000 C=0xFFEE1000
                                  (delta 0xE1000 = one 1280x720 luma plane),
                                  zero faults, panfrost GPU/MMU/job all zero
  logo channel disabled           panel BLACK, DECD still at 60 IRQ/s
  OSD repointed at DECD's IOVA    panel went WHITE -- it responded

That last line is the control that the 0069 run could not produce. The panel,
the raster and the IOMMU all work; DECD's buffer is fetchable through
translation; and the frame is genuinely red (Y=0x81, U=0x5A, V=0xF0, textbook
BT.601). So the black is specifically DECD's output not being routed, not a
wedged engine, a bad address, or an empty buffer.

Throughout all of it:

  0x05600010 = 0x03000010    video source 0: enable[1:0] = 0, format[15:8] = 0

Frames flowing at vsync, addresses translated exactly as stock's hardware sees
them at playback, and all three ARM-side resume steps reproduced -- and the
video source is still neither enabled nor given a pixel format. Stock's only
writer of that field, dec_reg_video_channel_attr_config(), is dead code in
decd.ko. Nothing ARM-side programs it, and this run shows nothing ARM-side can
make it unnecessary either. The MIPS firmware fed by DECD's VideoInfo dma-buf
is the only candidate left, and U-Boot parks it on every boot tested.

ONE MORE RULE, now reproducible. A single IOMMU page fault on master 2 wedges
the AFBD fetch engine for the rest of the boot. Seen on the 0069 boot, and
again here: the OSD control ran off the end of the 1.38 MB DECD mapping
(fault at 0xFFF52000, one page past the buffer end at 0xFFF51800) and the panel
stuck white -- the logo could not be restored by any register write, only by a
reboot. Budget one fault per boot; design experiments so the fault comes last.

Signed-off-by: Christopher Stenger <chris.h.stenger@gmail.com>
---
diff --git a/drivers/iommu/sun50i-iommu.c b/drivers/iommu/sun50i-iommu.c
--- a/drivers/iommu/sun50i-iommu.c
+++ b/drivers/iommu/sun50i-iommu.c
@@ -27,5 +27,6 @@
 #include <linux/spinlock.h>
 #include <linux/types.h>
 
+#include "dma-iommu.h"
 #include "iommu-pages.h"
 
@@ -729,7 +730,20 @@
 	sun50i_domain = kzalloc(sizeof(*sun50i_domain), GFP_KERNEL);
 	if (!sun50i_domain)
 		return NULL;
 
+	/*
+	 * Bind the instance now rather than waiting for attach_domain().
+	 * The core builds a device's IOMMU_RESV_DIRECT mappings from
+	 * iommu_device_register(), which runs before any device has
+	 * attached, and both sun50i_table_flush() and the page-table
+	 * allocator dereference sun50i_domain->iommu. Without this a
+	 * reserved direct mapping NULL-derefs in sun50i_iommu_map()
+	 * during probe. There is one IOMMU instance on this SoC and
+	 * attach_domain() assigns the same pointer, so this is strictly
+	 * an earlier assignment of the same value.
+	 */
+	sun50i_domain->iommu = sun50i_iommu_from_dev(dev);
+
 	sun50i_domain->dt =
 		iommu_alloc_pages_sz(GFP_KERNEL | GFP_DMA32, DT_SIZE);
 	if (!sun50i_domain->dt)
@@ -913,6 +927,7 @@
 static const struct iommu_ops sun50i_iommu_ops = {
 	.identity_domain = &sun50i_iommu_identity_domain,
 	.device_group	= generic_single_device_group,
 	.domain_alloc_paging = sun50i_iommu_domain_alloc_paging,
+	.get_resv_regions = iommu_dma_get_resv_regions,
 	.of_xlate	= sun50i_iommu_of_xlate,
 	.probe_device	= sun50i_iommu_probe_device,
diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun50i-h713.dtsi b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun50i-h713.dtsi
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun50i-h713.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun50i-h713.dtsi
@@ -114,6 +114,17 @@
 		framebuf_reserved: uboot-scanout@6c100000 {
 			reg = <0x6c100000 0x800000>;
 			no-map;
+			/*
+			 * Identity-map this into master 2's domain. The
+			 * display is adopted from U-Boot and is still
+			 * fetching this range by physical address, so a
+			 * translated master 2 faults on it and wedges the
+			 * AFBD fetch engine. IOVA == reg base and the
+			 * lengths match, which is what makes the core
+			 * class this IOMMU_RESV_DIRECT rather than a bare
+			 * reservation. Inert unless dec@5600000 is enabled.
+			 */
+			iommu-addresses = <&dec 0x6c100000 0x800000>;
 		};
 	};
 
@@ -1663,6 +1674,6 @@
 			resets = <&ccu RST_BUS_DISP>;
 			reset-names = "rst_bus_disp";
-			memory-region = <&decd_reserved>;
+			memory-region = <&decd_reserved>, <&framebuf_reserved>;
 			/*
 			 * Master 2, translation on -- the port of stock's
 			 * /dev/ge2d ioctl 0x4681, which is exactly
